There are several books being published by Addison-Wesley this year that
might help:

1. "Object-Oriented Programming:  An Evolutionary Approach, 2nd Ed."
   by Cox and Novobilski.  We updated the second edition to give
   better coverage of the language features and available tools.

2. "Objective-C:  Object-Oriented Programming Techniques" by Pinson
   and Wiener (Addison-Wesley).  The catalog says that the book presents 
   basic concepts, an accurate description of Objective-C (both Stepstone's 
   and NeXT's) and illustrates programming in Objective-C through examples.

3. "An Introduction to Object-Oriented Programming" by Budd (Addison-
   Wesley).  This book has been recently talked about in the comp.lang.c++ 
   as providing a general intro to OOP in a language independent manner 
   (ie, it talks about C++, Smalltalk, Objective-C, and Object Pascal).

4. There are also several case study style books that have small sections
   on objective-c.

You might also look into books that talk about Smalltalk programming,
since Objective-C's treatment of the concept of object is almost the
same.  Specifically, do a literature search on Model/View/Controller
since it is a very powerful way to organize and implement object-
based systems.

Finally, I would also be happy to send a copy of the Objective-C source 
code from my Aug/Sep JOOP article to anyone who would like it.  (This is 
the same code that was available last fall).  We are not on the internet, 
so if anyone would like to place that code into an archive somewhere thats 
appropriate, please let me know.

